export (
    Client, Config, defaultConfig,
    policy, limits, roots, follow, connectLimit, idlePerOrigin, idleTotal,
    client, close,
    Exchange, Received(..), exchange, exchangeUnbounded, next, send, sendUnbounded, sendUpgrade, sendUpgradeUnbounded,
    redirect, expectStatus, hostField, contentLength, retryAllowed,
)

-- An HTTP/1.1 client: requests sent over a pool of connections, each one
-- reaching only what a `ConnectPolicy` allows.
--
--     let c = Client.client(Client.defaultConfig)?
--     send(c, request(GET, "https://api.example.com/users/1"), Duration.seconds(10), fun(resp) {
--         expectStatus(resp, OK)?
--         receive(resp.body, 1048576)
--     })
--
-- **The response is the callback's.** `send` calls `use` with the response
-- once its head has arrived, and the body can be read only inside `use`.
-- When `use` returns, what it left unread is read and thrown away if that is
-- cheap (the limits' `drainBytes`), so the connection can carry the next request;
-- otherwise the connection is closed. A body read after `use` returned fails
-- with `Transport` of an `IOError` whose kind is `Closed`. `use`'s result,
-- and its error, are `send`'s.
--
-- **Every head, pulled.** `exchange` calls `use` with the exchange instead,
-- and `next` returns each head the server responds with: every interim (1xx)
-- response as `Informational`, then the final one as `Final`, whose body is
-- read as `send`'s is. A proxy relays a 103 that way. `send` is `exchange`
-- with the interim responses skipped. A 100 Continue is never returned: the
-- server sends it in response to the client's own `Expect`. A server may
-- send at most 16 interim responses before the final one; more, or a 101 to
-- a request that asked for no upgrade, is `BadResponse`.
--
-- **Errors** come back through `FromHTTP`, so `?` lifts them into the
-- caller's own error type. A request that got no response is `SendFailed`
-- of a `ClientError`, which records whether the server may have seen it:
-- `NotSent`, `MaybeSent` or `BadResponse`.
--
-- **Deadlines.** The timeout covers everything: resolving, connecting, the
-- TLS handshake, sending, the response's heads, every redirect `send`
-- follows, the one retry, and each read of the body inside `use`. The
-- `...Unbounded` calls have no total limit, for a transfer whose length
-- cannot be known ahead. Two limits hold for both: resolving, connecting and
-- the handshake together within the connect limit (30 s), and the limits'
-- `stall` (30 s), which closes a connection on which a read or write has waited
-- that long while no byte moved either way. A deadline that passes part-way
-- through a message closes the connection.
--
-- **Connections** are pooled per origin -- scheme, host and port -- and a
-- connection is reused only once both directions ended exactly: the request
-- was written whole, and its response was read to its exact end with
-- nothing after it. A byte past the end would be read as the next response,
-- so a connection that has one is closed. Up to 64 idle connections are kept
-- per origin and 100 in all, the oldest closed first past that, each for up
-- to the limits' `idleTimeout` (60 s). A `Client` is safe to share between
-- tasks.
--
-- **One retry.** A pooled connection may be stale: closed by the server
-- while it was idle, which the client learns only by using it, when it
-- ends before any byte of a response. Then the request is sent once more
-- on a new connection, if it is safe to: it was `NotSent`, or it was
-- `MaybeSent` and its method is idempotent or it has an `Idempotency-Key`;
-- and its body can be sent again. Nothing else is retried.
--
-- **Redirects** are responses like any other: a 3xx reaches `use` as it is.
-- `redirect(resp, req)` builds the request that follows one, under the rules
-- its comment gives, so a program that must see each hop loops itself.
-- A client built with `follow(n)` has `send` run that loop for up to `n` hops; the hop
-- past `n` is `NotSent(BadRedirect(TooManyRedirects))`, and one `redirect`
-- refuses for a reason other than `NotRedirect`, `NoLocation` or
-- `NotReplayable` is `NotSent(BadRedirect(...))`. Each hop goes through
-- `Net.resolve` and the policy again, as any request does. A response's
-- `url` is the URL it came from, and its `timings` how long each step of its
-- hop took.
--
-- **Upgrades.** `sendUpgrade` offers to switch the connection to another
-- protocol (WebSocket, for one), and on a 101 hands `use` the connection as a
-- `Duplex`: bytes the server sent after its 101 are read first, and
-- `shutdownWrite` ends this side's output while the server goes on. A 101
-- must name only protocols the request offered, or it is `BadResponse`. The
-- timeout ends with the 101; after it, the new protocol keeps its own time,
-- with the deadlines `use` sets, and the stall limit no longer applies. The
-- connection is closed when `use` returns, never pooled. A proxy calls the
-- server's `switch` inside `use`, so both connections close together.
--
-- **Bodies.** A request's method carries its body as a `Payload`, made by
-- `newPOST`, `newPUT`, `newPATCH` or `newExtension` from any `Body`
-- (`Net.HTTP.Body`), which holds its length and whether it can be sent
-- again. A `Stream` body sends the trailers its function returns, and a
-- relayed `Incoming` the trailers it came with. A body that fails, or does
-- not match the length it declared, fails the request, and the connection
-- is closed. A response's trailers are `trailers(resp.body)` once its body
-- has been read to the end.
--
-- **On the wire.** HTTP/1.1, one request at a time per connection, never
-- pipelined. The client writes `Host` (`hostField`), the framing
-- (`Content-Length` from the body's length, `contentLength`, or chunked when
-- it has none), `Expect: 100-continue`, and nothing else of its own. A
-- request body is written by a task of its own while the response is read,
-- so a response the server sends before reading the whole body (a 413) is
-- read.
-- TLS offers ALPN `http/1.1`.
--
-- **`Expect: 100-continue`** goes with a body of 1 MiB or more, and with a
-- relayed `Incoming` whose sender waits for a 100 itself. The body is sent
-- once the server's 100 arrives, or after 1 s without one; a final response
-- first means it is not sent, and the connection is closed. A relayed body
-- is read only when it is sent, and that first read is what sends its
-- sender's 100, so a server's refusal reaches the sender before it has sent
-- a byte.

